[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: The HTTP web service (Apache) on the internal server srv1 (10.5.0.4).
- Mechanism: Office PCs resolve internal names correctly, but the web daemon on srv1 is not listening on TCP/80 (or 443). As a result, browsers receive a connection refusal and render “site can’t be found/can’t be reached,” which is often misinterpreted as a DNS issue.
- Localization: Host srv1, interface eth0 (10.5.0.4). On clients (e.g., pc1), DNS is set to use 10.5.0.4 and resolves srv1.jeremysitlab.com to 10.5.0.4 (nslookup succeeds; ping resolves and reaches 10.5.0.4). However, HTTP requests fail: from pc1, curl http://srv1.jeremysitlab.com and curl http://10.5.0.4 return “Connection refused.” On srv1, no sockets are listening on :80/:443, while UDP/TCP :53 is actively served by named (DNS). systemctl shows apache2.service loaded but inactive (dead).
- Root cause: The web server on srv1 (Apache) is stopped, leaving no HTTP listener for internal sites hosted there.
- Corrective fix: Restart and enable the web service on srv1 (e.g., systemctl start apache2; systemctl enable apache2) and verify it listens on 0.0.0.0:80 (and 443 if used). After starting, re-test from a client (curl or browser to http://srv1.jeremysitlab.com). Note: DNS is functioning for internal names; no DNS change is required. If external name resolution is needed via srv1, enable/permit recursion or provide an external resolver, but that is separate from the current outage. 
=== END ===